<%# The House card's one piece of fine print, and it has been rewritten twice.

    Phase 3 said the model "overstates certainty". Phase 10 replaced that with
    a count — "four of the five correlated error components 538's House model
    uses" — which was factually sourced (the five-way decomposition and its
    values are on the methodology page, with citations) but framed the wrong
    thing: it reads as though 538's model were a specification we had taken on
    and not finished, one item outstanding. This site has never claimed to
    reproduce anyone's model. It borrows published values where they are
    published and says so on the methodology page; that is a different promise
    from parity, and a figure a reader meets on the dashboard should not imply
    the stronger one.

    So this states a property of OUR model and the consequence a reader
    actually needs — the number is a little too confident — and leaves the
    comparison, the arithmetic and the measured range to the methodology page,
    where they carry their citations and a date.

    It also sits below the histogram now rather than between the probability
    and the chart, where it split the card's one idea in half. Small print
    belongs with the other small print. %>
<p class="mt-2 text-xs text-slate-400" data-testid="house-error-note">
  Our error model leaves out one correlated term, so this figure is slightly firmer than it should be &mdash;
  <%= link_to "Known limitations", "#{methodology_path}#known-limitations", class: "underline decoration-dotted hover:text-slate-600" %>.
</p>
